mtproto-engine: build and pass its tests on Windows, Windows 7 included,
abort on a panic on an engine thread, and give the host explicit HTTP
addresses, host-ordered dependents and reset causes (ABI 6).

Upstream: https://github.com/TelegramMessenger/mtproto-engine
Branch: main
Revision: da4b2170210aab0430c4ff18c032991f3030cf45

Apply with `git apply` right after checking out that revision.

Builds for i686-win7-windows-msvc and x86_64-win7-windows-msvc (std built
from source with -Z build-std and +crt-static) and aarch64-pc-windows-msvc.
Every hunk is Windows-gated, or moves a test helper unchanged off Windows,
except three changes, made for every platform. First, the engine's threads
(mtproto-main, mtproto-worker-N, mtproto-resolver) run under an
abort-on-panic guard, so a host built with panic = "unwind" ends the
process on a panic there, as the engine's own panic = "abort" profile does,
instead of going on without the thread. Where the engine already aborts on
panic nothing changes. Tests:
mtproto-engine engine_thread::tests::a_panic_on_an_engine_thread_aborts_the_process,
mtproto-engine engine_thread::tests::a_panic_payload_that_panics_on_drop_still_aborts_the_process.
On Windows run the suites with libtest's serial mode:
  cargo test -p mtproto-core -p mtproto-engine -p mtproto-ffi -- --test-threads=1

Second, ABI 6 (mt_engine_abi_version() returns 6; no struct layout
changes) gives the host three capabilities and one fix. Explicit HTTP
candidates: mt_session_set_http_addresses names a session's plain HTTP
addresses, each at its own port, instead of deriving them from its
addresses at http_port; an empty list allows no plain HTTP. Host-ordered
dependents: a request with invoke_after and
MTRequestFlagSurfaceDependencyWaits (1 << 8) ends in MTEventKindFailed
with the server's code and text on MSG_WAIT_TIMEOUT or MSG_WAIT_FAILED,
instead of waiting for its dependency and going again. A request whose
invoke_after names one failed as 500 TEMP_KEY_ROTATED by a key change, or
refused this way, is refused before it goes out: with the flag as a local
400 MSG_WAIT_FAILED, each time, also once the named request went again and
completed, so the host holds it while it has that request and then sends it
without invoke_after; without the flag as 500 TEMP_KEY_ROTATED, as before.
Reset causes: MTEventKindUpdatesReset (13) gives its cause in code,
MTUpdatesResetServerSession (1), MTUpdatesResetLocalSession (2),
MTUpdatesResetUpdatesLost (3) or MTUpdatesResetUpdatesTooLong (4), and
for 1 carries the server's own new_session_created message, as received,
in its payload. The fix: the initConnection hash stored
(MTEventKindInitHashStored) is that of the environment the answered
request was sent with; an answer to a request sent with an environment
replaced since stores nothing, so the next request carries initConnection
with the current one. The test server answers MSG_WAIT_FAILED to a query
chained after a failed one, as Telegram does. Code that matches on
UpdatesReset, the ABI version checks, the fuzz targets and
docs/swift-integration.md follow. Tests:
mtproto-engine session_runtime::http::tests::the_hosts_http_addresses_replace_the_derived_routes,
mtproto-engine host_endpoints::an_address_only_in_the_tcp_list_gets_no_http,
mtproto-engine host_endpoints::the_hosts_http_addresses_keep_their_own_ports,
mtproto-engine host_endpoints::an_empty_http_list_allows_no_fallback,
mtproto-engine host_endpoints::a_new_http_list_moves_http_to_it,
mtproto-engine host_endpoints::the_same_http_list_again_changes_nothing_and_a_new_one_reconnects,
mtproto-engine host_endpoints::a_paused_session_connects_only_after_unpause_to_the_latest_lists,
