Third, mt_session_set_obfuscation_dc_id on a PFS session drops the
temporary key in use at once, failing the calls the server may have as
500 TEMP_KEY_ROTATED and keeping the queued ones, instead of at the next
drive, after the rest of the host's commands. An
mt_session_offer_temporary_key that follows finds no key in use and is
taken, also when a move to the media class and back arrives in one batch:
before, the offer of the key the session still talked under was ignored,
and a handshake replaced that key. Tests:
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::a_move_to_the_media_class_and_back_in_one_batch_takes_the_regular_key_again,
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::a_move_to_the_media_class_and_back_in_one_batch_without_a_media_key_takes_the_regular_key_again,
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::a_move_to_the_media_class_and_back_with_drives_between_takes_each_offered_key,
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::offers_wait_while_the_key_is_in_use_and_a_class_change_forgets_them,
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::a_move_to_the_media_class_and_back_refuses_the_key_bound_to_another_permanent_key,
mtproto-engine session_runtime::tests::a_move_to_the_media_class_and_back_during_a_handshake_takes_the_offered_keys,
mtproto-engine pfs_host_offers::a_move_to_the_media_class_and_back_takes_the_offered_regular_key_again.

Tests gated off on Windows, each with its reason in the source:
- mtproto-engine interface::tests::loopback_resolves_to_lo0 (Windows):
  Windows names no interface for an address, and its loopback is not lo0.
- mtproto-core sec_rows_zeroize::obfuscation_keys_and_keystreams_leave_no_copy_in_freed_memory,
  mtproto-core sec_rows_zeroize::a_transport_stream_leaves_no_proxy_secret_or_obfuscation_key_in_freed_memory,
  mtproto-core sec_rows_zeroize::a_fake_tls_stream_leaves_no_proxy_key_in_freed_memory,
  mtproto-engine sec_rows_zeroize::a_session_with_an_address_secret_leaves_no_secret_in_freed_memory,
  mtproto-engine sec_rows_zeroize::a_session_through_a_fake_tls_mtproxy_leaves_no_proxy_key_in_freed_memory
  (Windows x86_64 only): aes 0.8.4's autodetect Aes256 writes and wipes only
  the 480-byte AES-NI half of its 960-byte union on x86_64, so the unused
  half keeps stack bytes holding key material. They run on i686.

